in the human eye there are three color  receptors the colors we see are a  mixture of signals from these receptors  integrated by the brain the mantis  shrimp does things a little differently  these colorful coral dwellers have 12  color receptors but that doesn't mean  they see four times as many colors as we  do researchers figured this out by  training a mantis to grab for food tied  to tiny colored lights mantis shrimp  were not very good at discriminating  between lights that were very close in  color they could detect about a twenty  five nanometer difference in wavelength  basically they can tell pure orange from  pure yellow our eyes can perceive a four  nanometer difference in wavelength the  mantis shrimp scan their environment and  take quick yes-no readings  instead of integrating signals from  their color receptors like we do they  can speedily identify colors but they  don't tune the readings by comparing  input from different receptors this is a  fundamentally different way of seeing  color but it doesn't let them see more  colors in case you're starting to doubt  the amazingness of the mantis shrimp  have no fear in addition to color vision  they can also see UV light and two kinds  of polarized light  you
 

